    {
      "id": 301,
      "title": "How to improve RHR and HRV",
      "type": "row",
      "collapsed": true,
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 14,
        "w": 24,
        "h": 1
      },
      "panels": [
        {
          "id": 28,
          "title": "Benchmarks by age",
          "type": "text",
          "gridPos": {
            "x": 0,
            "y": 15,
            "w": 8,
            "h": 10
          },
          "options": {
            "mode": "markdown",
            "content": "### Benchmarks by age\n\n| Age | RHR (bpm) | HRV RMSSD (ms) |\n|-----|-----------|----------------|\n| 20-29 | 56-65 | 60-90 |\n| 30-39 | 58-67 | 45-70 |\n| 40-49 | 59-68 | 35-55 |\n| 50-59 | 60-69 | 25-45 |\n| 60+ | 60-70 | 20-35 |\n\nRHR below 60 = athletic. Above 80 = warrants attention.\n\nHRV declines ~2-3% per year naturally after 25. Your personal trend matters more than the absolute number: a drop of 10+ ms below your 30-day average is a reliable signal of incomplete recovery or illness onset.\n\n**Sources:** HRV ranges: [Sammito et al. (2021), European Heart Journal Digital Health](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34447956) (most recent systematic review; earlier reference: [Shaffer & Ginsberg (2017)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29034226)) | RHR ranges: derived from population surveys (NHANES, Framingham Heart Study) - no single normative paper; [Tverdal et al. (2008)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18285398) shows why low RHR matters (mortality association, not norms)"
          },
          "fieldConfig": {
            "defaults": {},
            "overrides": []
          }
        },
        {
          "id": 29,
          "title": "How to lower RHR",
          "type": "text",
          "gridPos": {
            "x": 8,
            "y": 15,
            "w": 8,
            "h": 10
          },
          "options": {
            "mode": "markdown",
            "content": "### How to lower RHR\n\n**Biggest levers (in order of impact):**\n\n1. **Aerobic fitness** - zone 2 cardio 150+ min/week (running, cycling, swimming). The single most effective intervention. A fit 50-year-old typically has a lower RHR than a sedentary 30-year-old.\n2. **Consistent sleep** - 7-9h at fixed times. Sleep debt raises resting HR by 3-5 bpm.\n3. **Reduce alcohol** - even 1-2 drinks elevates RHR the following night.\n4. **Stress management** - chronic cortisol keeps baseline HR elevated.\n5. **Hydration** - dehydration acutely raises HR 5-8 bpm.\n\nExpect 6-12 weeks to see meaningful change from training."
          },
          "fieldConfig": {
            "defaults": {},
            "overrides": []
          }
        },
        {
          "id": 30,
          "title": "How to raise HRV",
          "type": "text",
          "gridPos": {
            "x": 16,
            "y": 15,
            "w": 8,
            "h": 10
          },
          "options": {
            "mode": "markdown",
            "content": "### How to raise HRV\n\n**Biggest levers (in order of impact):**\n\n1. **Aerobic fitness** - same zone 2 training. Directly trains parasympathetic nervous system tone.\n2. **Sleep schedule consistency** - irregular sleep times suppress HRV even at adequate total duration.\n3. **Alcohol cutoff** - alcohol suppresses HRV the same night, reliably. Even moderate amounts.\n4. **Caffeine timing** - cutoff 6h before sleep; late caffeine fragments sleep architecture and reduces HRV.\n5. **Cold exposure** - cold showers or ice baths acutely boost HRV; consistent practice builds baseline.\n6. **Slow breathing** - 4-7s inhale / 6-8s exhale activates the vagus nerve. 5-10 min/day raises HRV measurably within weeks.\n\nSingle-day HRV is noisy - use the 7-day rolling average on this dashboard, not individual readings."
          },
          "fieldConfig": {
            "defaults": {},
            "overrides": []
          }
        }
      ]
    },
