    {
      "id": 102,
      "title": "How to improve sleep",
      "type": "row",
      "collapsed": true,
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 37,
        "w": 24,
        "h": 1
      },
      "panels": [
        {
          "id": 33,
          "title": "Sleep hygiene",
          "type": "text",
          "gridPos": {
            "x": 0,
            "y": 38,
            "w": 8,
            "h": 10
          },
          "options": {
            "mode": "markdown",
            "content": "### Sleep hygiene\n\n1. **Fixed wake time** - same time every day including weekends. This is the single highest-leverage habit. It anchors your circadian rhythm and builds sleep pressure reliably.\n2. **No screens 1h before bed** - blue light suppresses melatonin onset by 1-3 hours. Use blue-light glasses or night mode if unavoidable.\n3. **Dark, cool room** - 16-19C is optimal. Core body temperature must drop ~1C to initiate sleep; a cool room accelerates this.\n4. **No alcohol** - alcohol induces sleep but fragments it severely in the second half of the night. REM is suppressed even at 1-2 drinks.\n5. **Caffeine cutoff at 14:00** - caffeine half-life is 5-7h. A coffee at 15:00 still has half its caffeine at 21:00.\n6. **Get sunlight before 10:00** - 10-30 min of morning light sets your circadian clock and advances melatonin onset to the correct evening window."
          },
          "fieldConfig": {
            "defaults": {},
            "overrides": []
          }
        },
        {
          "id": 34,
          "title": "Sleep stages: what to optimise",
          "type": "text",
          "gridPos": {
            "x": 8,
            "y": 38,
            "w": 8,
            "h": 10
          },
          "options": {
            "mode": "markdown",
            "content": "### Sleep stages: what to optimise\n\n**Deep sleep (slow-wave)** - physically restorative, memory consolidation, growth hormone release. Mostly in the first half of the night.\n- Boosted by: exercise (especially aerobic), consistent wake time, cold room, avoiding alcohol.\n- Suppressed by: alcohol, sedatives, late eating.\n\n**REM sleep** - emotional regulation, creativity, procedural memory. Mostly in the second half of the night.\n- Boosted by: longer sleep (REM is last in the cycle), stress reduction, consistent schedule.\n- Suppressed by: alcohol, most sleep medications, sleep deprivation.\n\n**Light sleep** - transitional; not harmful but high ratios indicate sleep fragmentation.\n\nTarget rough proportions per night: 20-25% deep, 20-25% REM, 50-55% light, <5% awake."
          },
          "fieldConfig": {
            "defaults": {},
            "overrides": []
          }
        },
        {
          "id": 35,
          "title": "If you can't fall asleep",
          "type": "text",
          "gridPos": {
            "x": 16,
            "y": 38,
            "w": 8,
            "h": 10
          },
          "options": {
            "mode": "markdown",
            "content": "### If you can't fall asleep\n\n**Don't lie in bed awake** - bed becomes associated with wakefulness. After 20 min of lying awake, get up, do something calm in dim light, return when sleepy. This is stimulus control therapy, the most evidence-backed behavioural intervention for insomnia.\n\n**Sleep restriction** - counterintuitive but effective for chronic insomnia: temporarily restrict time in bed to match actual sleep time, then gradually expand. Builds sleep pressure. Do not nap.\n\n**4-7-8 breathing** - inhale 4s, hold 7s, exhale 8s. Activates parasympathetic nervous system. 3-4 cycles before sleep.\n\n**Don't clock-watch** - checking the time increases arousal and anxiety. Turn the clock away.\n\n**Avoid naps after 15:00** - naps reduce sleep pressure for the evening and make falling asleep harder.\n\n**Sources:** [Walker (2017) Why We Sleep](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6143346/) | [CBT-I overview (PubMed)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25454674/)"
          },
          "fieldConfig": {
            "defaults": {},
            "overrides": []
          }
        }
      ]
    },
